  <section class="rm-index not-content" aria-label="All topics on this roadmap">
    <h2 class="rm-index-title">All topics</h2>
    <p class="rm-index-lede">Every node on the roadmap above, as text. Each links to the page section that covers it.</p>
    <ul class="rm-index-list">
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading">Introduction</h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">What data engineering is, how it differs from data science, and what skills are needed.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/what-is-data-engineering/">What is Data Engineering?</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Designing, building, and maintaining pipelines that move and transform data at scale.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-engineer-vs-data-scientist/">Data Engineering vs Data Science</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">DE builds the infrastructure; DS uses it — roles, responsibilities, and overlap.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/skills-and-responsibilities/">Skills and Responsibilities</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">SQL, Python, cloud platforms, pipeline design, data modeling, and system design.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-engineering-lifecycle/">Data Engineering Lifecycle</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Generation → Ingestion → Transformation → Serving — the full data journey.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/choosing-the-right-technologies/">Choosing the Right Technologies</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Trade-offs between managed services, open source, cost, and team skill sets.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading">Programming Skills</h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">Languages and algorithmic foundations every data engineer needs.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-1-foundations/python/">Python</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">The go-to language for pipelines, scripting, and data processing.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label">Java / Scala</span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">JVM languages powering Spark, Kafka, and Hadoop ecosystems.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label">Go</span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Increasingly used for high-performance data tooling and CLI utilities.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-1-foundations/data-structure/">Data Structures &amp; Algorithms</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Arrays, hash maps, graphs, sorting — foundational for performance-aware engineering.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading">CS &amp; DevOps Foundations</h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">The underlying systems knowledge that makes a great data engineer.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/roadmaps/git/">Git and GitHub</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Version control, branching strategies, pull requests, and code review workflows.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/roadmaps/linux/">Linux Basics</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Shell scripting, file permissions, process management, cron jobs.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/roadmaps/networking/">Networking Fundamentals</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">TCP/IP, HTTP/S, DNS, VPCs, firewalls — essential for distributed systems.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-4-advanced/system-design/">Distributed Systems Basics</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Replication, partitioning, consistency models, CAP theorem.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-lifecycle/">Data Lifecycle</a></h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">How data moves through a system — from raw generation to serving consumers.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-lifecycle/#data-generation">Data Generation</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Transactional systems, events, logs, sensors, and third-party APIs.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-lifecycle/#data-storage">Data Storage</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Object storage, block storage, file systems — choosing the right tier.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-lifecycle/#data-ingestion">Data Ingestion</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Pulling data into the platform — batch, streaming, CDC, and micro-batch.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-lifecycle/#transformation--serving">Data Serving</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Making data available — APIs, query engines, BI tools, ML feature stores.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading">Sources of Data</h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">Where data originates and the considerations for collecting it reliably.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-lifecycle/#data-generation">Databases</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Operational OLTP databases — MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle, SQL Server.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-lifecycle/#data-generation">APIs</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">REST, GraphQL, webhooks — pulling data from external services.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-lifecycle/#data-generation">Logs</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Application logs, access logs, audit trails — structured vs unstructured.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-lifecycle/#data-generation">Mobile Apps &amp; IoT</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">High-velocity, high-volume event streams from devices and applications.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-1-foundations/sql/">Data Normalization</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">1NF through 3NF — reducing redundancy in relational data sources.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-modeling/">Data Modelling Techniques</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">ER diagrams, dimensional modelling, data vault, and wide table patterns.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading">Database Fundamentals</h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">Core database concepts every data engineer must understand deeply.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-1-foundations/sql/relational-databases/">DBMS vs RDBMS</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Database Management Systems — relational vs non-relational, schemas, and data models.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-warehousing/">OLTP vs OLAP</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Write-optimised vs read-optimised systems — different schemas, indexes, and engines.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-1-foundations/sql/">Learn SQL</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">SELECT, JOINs, aggregations, window functions, CTEs — the lingua franca of data.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-1-foundations/sql/indexes/">Indexing</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">B-tree, hash, bitmap, and partial indexes — how they speed up queries.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-1-foundations/sql/transactions/">Transactions</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">ACID guarantees, isolation levels, and locking strategies.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-4-advanced/system-design/#distributed-systems-fundamentals">CAP Theorem</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Consistency, Availability, Partition Tolerance — and why you can only have two.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-4-advanced/system-design/#scalability--capacity-planning">Horizontal vs Vertical Scaling</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Scale-up vs scale-out trade-offs for databases and compute.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading">Relational Databases</h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">Production-grade RDBMS platforms used in data engineering stacks.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-1-foundations/sql/relational-databases/">MySQL</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Open-source RDBMS — widely used for operational workloads and data sources.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-1-foundations/sql/relational-databases/">PostgreSQL</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Advanced open-source DB with rich types, JSONB, and strong analytics support.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-1-foundations/sql/relational-databases/">MariaDB</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">MySQL-compatible fork with additional storage engines and features.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/cloud/aws-databases/#rds--aurora">Aurora DB</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">AWS-managed MySQL/PostgreSQL-compatible DB with high availability.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-1-foundations/sql/relational-databases/">Oracle</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Enterprise RDBMS common in large financial and operational systems.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-1-foundations/sql/relational-databases/">MS SQL Server</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Microsoft's enterprise DB — often the source for CDC pipelines.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-modeling/">Slowly Changing Dimensions (SCD)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Type 1, 2, 3, and 6 — tracking historical changes in dimension tables.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-modeling/">Star vs Snowflake Schema</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Dimensional modelling strategies for data warehouses and OLAP cubes.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading">NoSQL Databases</h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">Non-relational databases — document, column, graph, and key-value stores.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/databases/mongodb/">MongoDB</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Document store — flexible JSON-like schema, widely used for semi-structured data.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/databases/cassandra/">Cassandra</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Wide-column store — high write throughput, tunable consistency, multi-region.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/cloud/aws-databases/#caching-elasticache--memorydb">Redis</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">In-memory key-value store — caching, pub/sub, session management.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/cloud/aws-analytics/#governance--search">ElasticSearch</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Full-text search and log analytics engine built on Lucene.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/cloud/aws-databases/#purpose-built-databases">Neo4j</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Graph database — nodes, edges, Cypher query language.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/databases/bigtable/">BigTable / HBase</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Google's wide-column NoSQL — HBase is the open-source equivalent on HDFS.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/databases/dynamodb/">DynamoDB</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">AWS serverless key-value and document DB — millisecond latency at any scale.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/cloud/azure-databases/#cosmos-db">CosmosDB</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Azure's globally distributed multi-model database service.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-warehousing/">Data Warehouse</a></h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">Centralised analytical repositories optimised for read-heavy workloads.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-warehousing/">What is a Data Warehouse?</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Purpose-built for analytics — column-oriented storage, MPP architecture.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-warehousing/#warehouse-fundamentals--architecture">Data Warehousing Architectures</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Inmon vs Kimball, lambda vs kappa, hub-and-spoke vs data mesh.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/data-warehouses/bigquery/">Google BigQuery</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Serverless, highly scalable cloud data warehouse — pay per query.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/data-warehouses/snowflake/">Snowflake</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Cloud-native DW with separation of storage and compute, multi-cloud.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/data-warehouses/redshift/">Amazon Redshift</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">AWS columnar data warehouse — RA3 nodes, Spectrum for S3 querying.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-warehousing/#data-marts-metadata--enterprise-concepts">Data Mart</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Subject-oriented subset of a data warehouse — faster, focused analytics.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-governance/#classification--modern-governance-models">Data Mesh</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Decentralised, domain-oriented data ownership as a product.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-governance/#classification--modern-governance-models">Data Fabric</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Unified architecture connecting disparate data sources and consumers.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading">Data Lake</h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">Schema-on-read storage for raw data at any scale — structured, semi-structured, and unstructured.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/formats/delta/">Databricks Delta Lake</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Open table format adding ACID transactions and versioning to data lakes.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/formats/hudi/">Onehouse / Apache Hudi</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Upserts and incremental processing on data lakes — Hudi table format.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-lifecycle/#data-storage">Metadata-first Architecture</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Data catalogs, schema registries, and data discovery platforms.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/cloud/gcp-compute/#cloud-run--cloud-functions">Serverless Options</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">AWS Lake Formation, Athena, Glue — fully managed lake infrastructure.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading">Cloud Computing</h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">The platforms and services that power modern data infrastructure.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-4-advanced/system-design/#storage--database-selection">Cloud Architectures</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Multi-cloud, hybrid cloud, landing zones, and well-architected frameworks.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/cloud/aws/">AWS (EC2, S3, RDS)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Compute, object storage, and managed databases on Amazon Web Services.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/cloud/azure/">Azure (VMs, Blob, SQL DB)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Microsoft Azure equivalents — Virtual Machines, Blob Storage, Azure SQL.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/cloud/gcp/">GCP (Compute, GCS, Cloud SQL)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Google Cloud — Compute Engine, Cloud Storage, Cloud SQL, Dataflow.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading"><a href="/level-3-technologies/ingestion/ingestion-patterns/">Types of Data Ingestion</a></h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">Strategies for moving data from sources into the data platform.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/ingestion/ingestion-patterns/#batch-ingestion">Batch Ingestion</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Periodic bulk loads — nightly ETL jobs, daily snapshots, scheduled exports.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/ingestion/ingestion-patterns/#streaming-ingestion">Streaming Ingestion</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Continuous data flow — Kafka, Kinesis, Pub/Sub for real-time pipelines.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/ingestion/ingestion-patterns/#real-time--micro-batch">Realtime Ingestion</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Sub-second latency ingestion — event-driven architectures and CDC.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/ingestion/ingestion-patterns/#hybrid-architectures">Hybrid Ingestion</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Combining batch and streaming — lambda architecture patterns.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading">Data Pipelines</h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">Automated workflows that extract, transform, and load data reliably.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-lifecycle/#data-ingestion">Extract Data</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Reading from source systems — full loads vs incremental extracts.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-lifecycle/#transformation--serving">Transform Data</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Cleaning, normalising, aggregating, and enriching raw data.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-2-core-concepts/data-lifecycle/#data-ingestion">Load Data</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Writing to target systems — full replace vs upsert vs append strategies.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/cloud/azure-data-solutions/#data-factory--orchestration">Data Factory (ETL tools)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Azure Data Factory, AWS Glue, Fivetran — managed pipeline platforms.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/orchestration/airflow/">Apache Airflow</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Open-source workflow orchestration — DAGs, operators, sensors, hooks.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/transformation/dbt/">dbt</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Transform data in your warehouse using SQL — tests, documentation, lineage.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/orchestration/airflow/">Luigi / Prefect</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Alternative Python-native orchestration frameworks.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/orchestration/airflow/">Job Scheduling</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Cron, Airflow schedulers, event-driven triggers — managing pipeline runs.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading">Cluster Computing Basics</h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">Distributed compute concepts and tools for processing data at scale.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/hadoop/#hdfs-fundamentals--architecture">What is Cluster Computing</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Coordinating multiple nodes to process data in parallel.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/hadoop/#hdfs-fundamentals--architecture">Distributed File Systems</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">HDFS, cloud object storage — how data is split and replicated across nodes.</span></li>
